"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[22206],{422206:e=>{e.exports="# DAX → Metric-View YAML Pattern Catalog\n\nEach entry shows the DAX pattern, its UC metric-view-YAML equivalent, and migration notes. The system prompt's Phase C (Translate measures) reads this skill before authoring any measure expression. **Do not invent translation rules from memory** — if a measure's pattern is not covered here or in `dax-unsupported.md`, mark the measure `cannot_migrate` with reason `no_skill_rule` and continue.\n\n## How `source.` resolves\n\nEvery translation in this catalog uses `source.<column>` — that's the metric view's `source:` field at query time. Phase B of the system prompt binds each metric view's `source:` per `architecture-patterns.md` \xa79: the **raw upstream** (under `source_catalog.source_schema` or a user-supplied per-table mapping) only when the underlying Power BI table is PASSTHROUGH and has zero retained materializable calc columns (per \xa713.1 below); the **`v_<table>` source view** under `<target_catalog>.<target_schema>` in every other case (any non-trivial M-query transformation, embedded SQL, real-data `Table.FromRows`, period-pair unification, **or** at least one retained materializable \xa713.1 calc column).\n\nThis isn't an optimization detail — DAX measures, calculated columns, and relationships all execute against the post-M-query semantic-model layer in Power BI. The metric view's `source:` must reference the same row set, which is what `v_<table>` materializes when transformations or \xa713.1 calc columns exist. Pointing the metric view at the raw upstream when those apply yields silently wrong aggregations or query-time errors on missing columns. Pure-`RELATED` calc cols (\xa713.2) and untranslatable calc cols (\xa713.3) do **not** force `v_<table>` — the former become metric-view joins, the latter cascade aggregating measures to `lod_fixed` while the host stays PASSTHROUGH.\n\nThe same rule applies to `joins[].source:` (Pattern 7 — RELATED): a dimension table with a non-trivial M-query or at least one materializable (\xa713.1) DAX calculated column is referenced via its own `v_<dim>` source view, not its raw upstream — even when the fact table is PASSTHROUGH. Each table's bucket is decided independently.\n\n## 1. Direct aggregations (leaf measures)\n\n### SUM\n\n```\nDAX:  SUM(table[column])\nSQL:  SUM(source.column)\nYAML: expr: SUM(source.column)\n```\n\n### COUNT / COUNTROWS\n\n```\nDAX:  COUNTROWS(table)      or  COUNT(table[column])\nSQL:  COUNT(1)              or  COUNT(source.column)\n```\n\n### DISTINCTCOUNT\n\n```\nDAX:  DISTINCTCOUNT(table[column])\nSQL:  COUNT(DISTINCT source.column)\n```\n\n### AVERAGE / MIN / MAX\n\n```\nDAX:  AVERAGE(table[column])\nSQL:  AVG(source.column)\n```\n\nDirect 1:1 mapping for all standard aggregation functions. Apply the same shape to `MIN` / `MAX`.\n\n## 2. DIVIDE (safe division)\n\n```\nDAX:  DIVIDE([Numerator], [Denominator], 0)\nYAML: expr: |\n        CASE WHEN MEASURE(Denominator) = 0 THEN 0\n             ELSE MEASURE(Numerator) / MEASURE(Denominator)\n        END\n```\n\n`DIVIDE` returns the alternate result (3rd argument, default `BLANK`) when the denominator is zero. Map to `CASE WHEN`. The third argument is the fallback — if omitted in DAX, use `0` or wrap with `COALESCE` depending on the business intent.\n\n## 3. CALCULATE with FILTER\n\n```\nDAX:  CALCULATE(SUM(table[revenue]), FILTER(table, table[status] = \"Active\"))\nYAML: expr: SUM(source.revenue) FILTER (WHERE source.status = 'Active')\n```\n\nUC metric views support `FILTER (WHERE ...)` on individual aggregate expressions. This is the direct equivalent of `CALCULATE` with a simple `FILTER`.\n\n## 4. CALCULATE with ALL / REMOVEFILTERS\n\n```\nDAX:  CALCULATE([Total Revenue], ALL(dim_table))\n```\n\nThis removes all filter context from `dim_table`. In a metric view, this pattern is typically used for \"share of total\" calculations. Use **coarser LOD** with window measures (`range: all`):\n\n```yaml\nmeasures:\n  - name: total_revenue\n    expr: SUM(source.revenue)\n  - name: total_revenue_all\n    expr: SUM(source.revenue)\n    window:\n      - order: <dimension_to_remove>\n        range: all\n        semiadditive: last\n  - name: share_of_total\n    expr: MEASURE(total_revenue) / MEASURE(total_revenue_all)\n```\n\nWhen the `ALL(...)` argument scopes to a column or expression that can't be expressed as a window order, fall through to `dax-unsupported.md` (`ALLSELECTED` / `ALL` complex contexts).\n\n## 5. SWITCH / SELECTEDVALUE (slicer dispatch)\n\n```\nDAX:  SWITCH(\n        SELECTEDVALUE(dim_switch[value]),\n        \"Option A\", [Measure_A],\n        \"Option B\", [Measure_B],\n        [Measure_A]\n      )\n```\n\n**NOT a metric view measure.** This is display-layer logic — the user picks a slicer value and the visual changes which measure to show.\n\n**Migration strategy:** Define each option as a separate measure in the metric view. Let the dashboard / AI/BI slicer handle the toggle. See `architecture-patterns.md` \xa72.\n\n```yaml\nmeasures:\n  - name: Measure_A\n    expr: SUM(source.col_a)\n  - name: Measure_B\n    expr: SUM(source.col_b)\n```\n\nIn the AI/BI dashboard, bind a slicer widget to a parameter that drives **which widget** is shown (or which measure each widget displays via dataset parameters), not \"which measure is computed\".\n\n## 6. SWITCH(TRUE(), ...) context routing\n\n```\nDAX:  SWITCH(TRUE(),\n        [flag] = \"Current\", CALCULATE(SUM(t[val]), FILTER(t, t[period] = \"CY\")),\n        [flag] = \"Prior\",   CALCULATE(SUM(t[val]), FILTER(t, t[period] = \"PY\"))\n      )\n```\n\n**Migration strategy:** Replace the flag measure with a dimension. Each branch becomes a filtered measure or the user filters by the dimension at query time.\n\n```yaml\ndimensions:\n  - name: period\n    expr: source.period # \"CY\" or \"PY\"\n\nmeasures:\n  - name: value\n    expr: SUM(source.val)\n  # User queries: WHERE period = 'CY'\n```\n\n## 7. RELATED (cross-table column reference)\n\n```\nDAX:  RELATED(dim_table[attribute])\n```\n\nIn Power BI, `RELATED` follows a relationship to pull a column from a related table. In UC metric views, this is handled by **joins**:\n\n```yaml\njoins:\n  - name: dim\n    source: catalog.schema.dim_table\n    on: source.fk = dim.pk\n\ndimensions:\n  - name: attribute\n    expr: dim.attribute\n```\n\nThe `joins[]` block names each related table; subsequent `dimensions:` entries can reference `dim.column`. See `architecture-patterns.md` \xa78 (Dimension completeness).\n\n**The `source:` value follows the same rule as the metric view's top-level `source:`.** Pick by the dimension's bucket: `<target_catalog>.<target_schema>.v_<dim_table>` when the dimension's M-query has any non-trivial transformation or it carries at least one materializable (\xa713.1) calc column; the dimension's raw upstream qualified name otherwise (PASSTHROUGH-and-no-materializable-\xa713.1-calc-cols). Pure `RELATED` (\xa713.2) and untranslatable (\xa713.3) calc cols on the dim do not flip the dim's bucket. The fact table's bucket does not influence the dim's join source — each table is classified independently. See `architecture-patterns.md` \xa79.\n\n## 8. IF / IIF (conditional)\n\n```\nDAX:  IF([Revenue] > 1000, \"High\", \"Low\")\nSQL:  CASE WHEN MEASURE(Revenue) > 1000 THEN 'High' ELSE 'Low' END\n```\n\nDirect mapping to SQL `CASE WHEN`. `IIF` is just an alias for `IF`.\n\n## 9. FORMAT (display formatting)\n\n```\nDAX:  FORMAT([Revenue], \"$#,##0.00\")\n```\n\n**Not a measure.** Use semantic metadata instead:\n\n```yaml\nformat:\n  type: currency\n  currency_code: USD\n  decimal_places:\n    type: exact\n    places: 2\n```\n\nPower BI's `formatString:` field on a measure (e.g. `\"\\\"$\\\"#,0.00\"`) is the same idea — translate to the metric view's `format:` block where possible. Display-layer-only `FORMAT()` calls in DAX measure expressions almost always reduce to either dropping the formatting wrapper (let the dashboard widget format) or migrating the underlying numeric measure and applying `format:` metadata.\n\n## 10. Measure-to-measure reference\n\n```\nDAX:  [Gross Profit] / [Revenue]\nYAML: expr: MEASURE(Gross_Profit) / MEASURE(Revenue)\n```\n\nBracket references `[MeasureName]` in DAX become `MEASURE(measure_name)` in UC metric view YAML. The composability model is nearly identical. Whitespace in DAX measure names becomes underscores or backticks in metric view names — match what the metric-view-authoring skill specifies.\n\n## 11. BLANK() / null handling\n\n```\nDAX:  IF(ISBLANK([value]), 0, [value])\nSQL:  COALESCE(MEASURE(value), 0)\n```\n\nDAX `BLANK()` maps to SQL `NULL`. Use `COALESCE` or `CASE WHEN ... IS NULL`.\n\n## 12. VALUES / DISTINCT (table functions)\n\n```\nDAX:  COUNTROWS(VALUES(table[column]))\nSQL:  COUNT(DISTINCT source.column)\n```\n\n`VALUES` returns distinct values of a column. In aggregation context, `COUNTROWS(VALUES(...))` is equivalent to `COUNT(DISTINCT ...)`.\n\n## 13. Calculated columns\n\nDAX calculated columns are defined on a Power BI table and evaluated row-by-row **after** the M-query transformations apply, in **row context** — at each row, plain column references resolve to that row's value, and there is no filter context active. This is structurally different from measures, which evaluate in **filter context** (the set of slicer / page / `CALCULATE` / `FILTER` filters active when the visual queries them). The translation rules in patterns 1–12 above are written for measure (filter-context) DAX, and most of them do not survive a copy-paste into a calculated column. Calc-column translation is therefore a **strict subset** of the catalog, not a re-use of it.\n\nSee `pbi-schema.md` \xa7\"Calculated columns\" for the schema marker (`columns[].type === \"calculated\"`) and the table-bucket reclassification rule.\n\n```\nDAX (calculated column on Sales table):\n  Sales[ProfitMargin] = DIVIDE(Sales[Revenue] - Sales[Cost], Sales[Revenue], 0)\n\nDAX (measure that aggregates it):\n  [Avg Profit Margin] = AVERAGE(Sales[ProfitMargin])\n```\n\n### 13.1 Row-context-safe DAX → SQL derived column\n\nThe following calc-column DAX shapes translate cleanly to a SQL derived column inside `v_<table>`. Each operates on the current row and produces a scalar. **Only patterns in this list are safe.** Anything else falls into \xa713.3 below.\n\n| Calc-column DAX shape                                                                                                                                   | SQL derived column                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Same-row scalar arithmetic (`Sales[Revenue] - Sales[Cost]`, `Sales[Qty] * 1.0`)                                                                         | Plain SQL arithmetic over the upstream's columns.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| `IF(<row-scalar cond>, <row-scalar a>, <row-scalar b>)` / `IIF`                                                                                         | `CASE WHEN ... THEN ... ELSE ... END` (Pattern 8).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| `SWITCH(<row-scalar>, <key>, <val>, …)`                                                                                                                 | Chained `CASE WHEN`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DIVIDE(<row-scalar num>, <row-scalar den>, <row-scalar fallback>)`                                                                                     | `CASE WHEN den = 0 THEN fallback ELSE num/den END` (Pattern 2 shape, scalar args).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| `IF(ISBLANK(<col>), <fallback>, <col>)` / null-coalesce on row scalars                                                                                  | `COALESCE` (Pattern 11).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| Scalar text functions over row fields (`UPPER`, `LOWER`, `LEFT`, `RIGHT`, `LEN`, `CONCATENATE`/`&`, `TRIM`, `SUBSTITUTE` for literal-pair replacements) | Spark SQL equivalents (`UPPER`, `LOWER`, `SUBSTRING`, `LENGTH`, `CONCAT` / `\\|\\|`, `TRIM`, `REGEXP_REPLACE`).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| Scalar date / time over row fields — `YEAR(d)` / `MONTH(d)` / `DAY(d)`                                                                                  | `YEAR(d)` / `MONTH(d)` / `DAY(d)`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| `WEEKDAY(d)` (default `return_type=1`: Sun=1…Sat=7)                                                                                                     | `DAYOFWEEK(d)` — Spark returns Sun=1…Sat=7 by default, identical numbering. **No `- 1` offset.** For `WEEKDAY(d, 2)` (Mon=1…Sun=7) use `((DAYOFWEEK(d) + 5) % 7) + 1`; for `WEEKDAY(d, 3)` (Mon=0…Sun=6) use `(DAYOFWEEK(d) + 5) % 7`. Read the second arg of every `WEEKDAY` call before translating.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| `EOMONTH(d, months)` — end of the month `months` periods before/after `d`                                                                               | `LAST_DAY(ADD_MONTHS(d, months))`. Plain `LAST_DAY(d)` is correct _only_ when `months = 0`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| `EDATE(d, months)` — date `months` periods before/after `d`, same day-of-month                                                                          | `ADD_MONTHS(d, months)`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `DATEDIFF(d1, d2, <unit>)` (DAX)                                                                                                                        | DAX counts _interval-boundary crossings_; Spark 3-arg `DATEDIFF(<unit>, d1, d2)` counts _whole elapsed units_. The two functions diverge at **every** unit level whenever the inputs are not aligned to (truncated to) the requested unit. <br><br>**Examples of the divergence:** `DATEDIFF(\"2024-01-01 23:59:00\", \"2024-01-02 00:01:00\", DAY)` is `1` in DAX (one midnight boundary) and `0` in Spark (2 minutes elapsed); `DATEDIFF(\"2019-12-31\", \"2020-01-01\", YEAR)` is `1` in DAX and `0` in Spark; the same shape applies at HOUR / MINUTE / SECOND when timestamps straddle a unit boundary without elapsing a full unit. <br><br>**Safe direct mapping:** only when both inputs are explicitly aligned to the requested unit before the call (`DATE(d)` for DAY, `DATE_TRUNC('HOUR', d)` for HOUR, etc.) **or** when the calc column's intent is unambiguously whole-elapsed-unit. The two-argument Spark `DATEDIFF(end, start)` is days-only and flips arg order from DAX; the 3-arg `DATEDIFF(<unit>, d1, d2)` is documented for Databricks SQL / Databricks Runtime 10.4 LTS+ (the underscore variant `date_diff` is DBR 13.3+) — verify availability against the target warehouse / runtime if it is older than 10.4 LTS. <br><br>**Boundary-arithmetic alternatives** (correct regardless of alignment): <br> – `DAY` → `DATEDIFF(DATE_TRUNC('DAY', d2), DATE_TRUNC('DAY', d1))` (2-arg form on truncated dates) <br> – `HOUR` → `(DATEDIFF(DATE_TRUNC('DAY', d2), DATE_TRUNC('DAY', d1)) * 24) + (HOUR(d2) - HOUR(d1))` (or `unix_timestamp(date_trunc('HOUR', d2)) - unix_timestamp(date_trunc('HOUR', d1)) DIV 3600`) <br> – `MINUTE` / `SECOND` — analogous: truncate to unit, take the integer difference of the truncated epoch values divided by the unit-in-seconds <br> – `YEAR` → `YEAR(d2) - YEAR(d1)` <br> – `MONTH` → `(YEAR(d2) - YEAR(d1)) * 12 + (MONTH(d2) - MONTH(d1))` <br> – `QUARTER` → `(YEAR(d2) - YEAR(d1)) * 4 + (QUARTER(d2) - QUARTER(d1))` <br> – `WEEK` depends on DAX's start-of-week rule (Sunday by default) and rarely has a clean direct equivalent — verify intent with the user or skip the calc col with `lod_fixed`. <br><br>Do **not** translate as a bare `DATEDIFF(<unit>, d1, d2)` unless you can demonstrate the inputs are aligned to that unit. When in doubt, use the boundary-arithmetic form or skip with `lod_fixed`. |\n| `DATEVALUE(<text>)`                                                                                                                                     | `TO_DATE(<text>, <format>)`. Pass an explicit format string when the text isn't ISO-8601.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| Scalar date arithmetic `<row date> + <integer days>` (or subtraction)                                                                                   | `DATE_ADD(d, n)` / `DATE_SUB(d, n)`. Spark also accepts `d + INTERVAL n DAYS`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| **Not** `DATEADD(<dates>, n, <interval>)` (DAX)                                                                                                         | DAX `DATEADD` returns a _table_ of shifted dates — it's a time-intelligence function used inside `CALCULATE`, **not** a scalar. It is not equivalent to Spark `DATE_ADD` (despite the name). If a calc column wraps `DATEADD` to extract a scalar (e.g. inside `CALCULATE(MAX(...), DATEADD(...))`), the surrounding shape is filter-context (\xa713.3) — skip with `lod_fixed`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| Scalar logical / type funcs (`AND`, `OR`, `NOT`, `ISBLANK` over a row scalar, `BLANK()`)                                                                | `AND`, `OR`, `NOT`, `IS NULL`, `NULL`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| **Pure** `RELATED(other_table[col])` — covered in \xa713.2 below; **not** a derived column.                                                                | Migrated as a metric-view join, not as a derived column.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n\n```sql\n-- v_sales body (calc col Sales[ProfitMargin] is row-context-safe)\nSELECT\n  s.*,\n  CASE WHEN s.revenue = 0 THEN 0\n       ELSE (s.revenue - s.cost) / s.revenue END AS profit_margin\nFROM <upstream> s\n```\n\nThen any measure aggregating the calc column references the materialized name in filter context, translated normally per patterns 1–12:\n\n```yaml\nmeasures:\n  - name: avg_profit_margin\n    expr: AVG(source.profit_margin)\n```\n\n**Calc-column dependency order.** When one calc column references another within the same table (DAX `[OtherCalc]` reference), list them in dependency order in the source view's `SELECT` clause; SQL evaluates left-to-right within a `SELECT` projection.\n\n### 13.2 RELATED-based calc columns → metric-view join (not derived column)\n\nIf a calc column's DAX is _purely_ `RELATED(other_table[col])`, do **not** materialize it as a derived column in `v_<table>`. It is structurally a join attribute, not a per-row computation, and a denormalizing JOIN inside `v_<table>` ties the dim to one specific fact. Instead:\n\n- Add the related table as a `joins[]` entry on every metric view that needs the attribute (Pattern 7).\n- Expose the dim attribute via the metric view's `dimensions:` block (`expr: dim.col`).\n- The fact's `v_<table>` body adds nothing for this calc column.\n\nMixed shapes — `[X] = RELATED(Other[col]) * Sales[Qty]` — combine \xa713.1 and \xa713.2: the `RELATED` half ideally surfaces as a join-driven dimension and the same-row half stays in `v_<table>`, but the metric view's column model can only support this when the calc column is consumed as a _dimension_ (not aggregated). When a measure aggregates a mixed calc column, the only safe materialization is a denormalizing JOIN inside `v_<table>` (`SELECT s.*, c.col * s.qty AS x FROM <upstream> s JOIN <other_upstream> c ON s.fk = c.pk`); accept the denormalization or skip the measure with `lod_fixed`.\n\n### 13.3 Calc-column DAX that does NOT translate to a derived column\n\nAny of the shapes below carry semantics that a SQL derived column cannot express. **Treat each as `cannot_migrate` / `lod_fixed`** for every measure that aggregates the calc column. Document the omitted calc column where a downstream reader will see it:\n\n- If a `v_<table>` source view exists for the host (because the M-query is non-trivial **or** the host has at least one \xa713.1 calc col), document the omitted \xa713.3 column in `v_<table>`'s `comment:` so a downstream reader knows why it's missing from the source view's projection.\n- If the host is PASSTHROUGH with only \xa713.2 / \xa713.3 calc cols (no `v_<table>` exists), document the omitted column in the metric view's top-level YAML `comment:` per `SKILL.md` \xa7\"Skip Documentation Format\", and surface it in the Phase F final report.\n\nDo not invent translations.\n\n- **Aggregation functions** (`SUM`, `AVERAGE`, `COUNT`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `DISTINCTCOUNT`, etc.) inside a calc-column expression. In a calc col these inject \"no row filter\" filter context and produce the grand total at every row — almost never what the modeler intended; even when they did intend it, the translation is a window function (`SUM(...) OVER (PARTITION BY ...)`), not a scalar. A bespoke window-function rewrite is acceptable only when (a) the partition keys are explicit and the user can confirm the intent, and (b) the resulting query stays read-only inside `v_<table>`. When in doubt, skip.\n- **`CALCULATE` / `CALCULATETABLE`** inside a calc column — these manipulate filter context. Not row-context-translatable.\n- **`FILTER`, `ALL`, `ALLEXCEPT`, `REMOVEFILTERS`, `ALLSELECTED`** — table-valued / filter-context constructs.\n- **Measure references** `[OtherMeasure]` inside a calc column — a context-transition occurs (row context becomes filter context); the resulting value is filter-context-dependent, not a row-scalar.\n- **`EARLIER` / `EARLIEST`** — DAX-only constructs that walk the iteration stack of nested row contexts. No SQL equivalent.\n- **Table-valued ops** (`SUMX` / `AVERAGEX` / other iterators, `VALUES`, `DISTINCT` as a table arg, `TOPN`, `SELECTCOLUMNS` / `ADDCOLUMNS` inside a calc column).\n- **Multi-relationship `RELATED` chains** that traverse more than one active relationship, or any `RELATED` that depends on `USERELATIONSHIP`.\n- **Time-intelligence functions** (`TOTALYTD`, `SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR`, `DATESINPERIOD`, etc.) — see `dax-unsupported.md`.\n\nThe cascade rule is automatic: every measure that aggregates a \xa713.3 calc column is `cannot_migrate` with reason `lod_fixed`. The aggregating measure cannot succeed if the underlying column doesn't exist in `v_<table>`.\n"}}]);
